{
  "abstract": "When an agent generates several answers, how many pairwise model judgments does it need to return the same answer as an all-pairs judge? We study the highest-scoring producer under wins, ties and a declared tie priority. We separate the cost of discovering its identity from the cost of showing sufficient evidence once all outcomes are known. We reduce the minimum number of atomic judgments proving a winner to at most one weighted matching per candidate. For unequal prices, the winner’s adjusted lead limits which wins can be omitted. A second reduction gives randomized polynomial time in the explicit answer-text size for minimum candidate-byte cost. On 4,771 complete twelve-producer MixInstruct panels, adaptive acquisition reproduced every winner using 23.15 of 66 judgments on average. Hindsight certificates required 17.30. On 160 six-producer MT-Bench panels, the same policy reproduced every winner using 6.93 of 15 judgments under FastChat’s convention for position disagreements. This convention was recovered after an initial analysis covered only 18 panels. A five-comparison knockout already agreed on 147. In a secondary analysis of direct human votes, the full-panel answer earned 54.49–69.87% preference across the thirteen disagreements, allowing every completion of missing votes. Its question-bootstrap outer interval was 33.33–87.50%. The savings concern the judging stage. Choosing which decision is worth reproducing also requires evidence about the delivered answer.",
